1
00:00:02,910 --> 00:00:05,010
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,270 --> 00:00:05,690
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:09,010
My name is Catherine and we're on the road today.

4
00:00:09,010 --> 00:00:10,230
We're traveling a little bit.

5
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:10,710
That's right.

6
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:16,250
Today we have a lesson that was requested by one of our users and I think it's a very handy lesson.

7
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:19,430
If we're traveling, we are buying tickets at a train station.

8
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:19,950
That's right.

9
00:00:20,130 --> 00:00:22,310
So I personally love traveling by train.

10
00:00:22,490 --> 00:00:25,950
I think it's a very romantic way to see different countries, different places.

11
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:30,680
We're going to learn all about how to get tickets and how to talk about different parts of a train.

12
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,070
So let's first listen to our dialogue.

13
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:41,980
Hi, I would like to purchase a one-way ticket to Brussels, please.

14
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:43,120
Certainly, sir.

15
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:44,180
This is our train schedule.

16
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:50,440
We have an express train departing every morning and an overnight train that departs at 9 p.m.

17
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:51,480
How long does it take to get there?

18
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:52,820
About 12 hours.

19
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:57,160
We currently have tickets available only for first class on the express train.

20
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:01,560
If you'd like, you can choose a sleeper on the overnight train, which is a bit less expensive.

21
00:01:02,510 --> 00:01:03,990
Yeah, I think that's the best option.

22
00:01:04,470 --> 00:01:05,510
Do you serve food on a train?

23
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:07,230
12 hours is such a long time.

24
00:01:07,490 --> 00:01:08,290
Yes, of course.

25
00:01:08,630 --> 00:01:12,010
There is a dining car towards the end of the train where they serve meals at all times.

26
00:01:12,530 --> 00:01:15,550
We do provide complimentary water and coffee for all of our passengers.

27
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:16,310
Great.

28
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:17,070
I'll take it.

29
00:01:17,410 --> 00:01:18,010
Here you are, sir.

30
00:01:18,670 --> 00:01:21,450
Your train leaves from platform number 9 at 9 on the dot.

31
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:26,570
Remember to be here at least 30 minutes before your scheduled departure time or else you might miss your train.

32
00:01:26,870 --> 00:01:27,310
I understand.

33
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:28,850
Thank you very much.

34
00:01:29,130 --> 00:01:29,750
Have a great trip.

35
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:35,480
All right, we're back.

36
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:40,410
So now we're going to take a look at a couple of words that we've picked out for you on Language Takeaway.

37
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:45,400
Language Takeaway.

38
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:49,890
So there are many different kinds of tickets you can purchase, you can buy for your train journey.

39
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:53,230
The first time we're going to talk about is maybe the fastest.

40
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:55,210
This is called an express train.

41
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:56,030
That's right.

42
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:59,850
So as the name says, it's an express train.

43
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:01,910
That means it's a very fast train.

44
00:02:02,770 --> 00:02:09,130
And it depends because in different places, sometimes they call it the fast train, sometimes they call it the express train.

45
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:12,270
But basically the idea is that it's a very fast train.

46
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,980
And it's fast usually because it stops very few times.

47
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:22,220
For example, if we want to go on the Paris to Berlin train, it won't stop in every single town.

48
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:24,740
It will probably only stop a couple of times.

49
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:25,300
Exactly.

50
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:32,660
So apart from the express trains that usually they'll go in the daytime, we have an overnight train.

51
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:38,340
Overnight trains are very fun, or at least they can be fun if you have a comfortable place to sleep.

52
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:44,800
Basically the idea is you get on the train at night and you sleep on the train and you get off in the morning.

53
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:45,520
That's right.

54
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:55,860
It's actually a very popular way of traveling, especially if you're on a budget because you're basically saving yourself one night of spending the night at a hotel.

55
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:56,780
That's right.

56
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:00,860
So you're going to have to find a place to sleep if you're taking the overnight train.

57
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:02,260
There are usually different options.

58
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:06,700
The most uncomfortable is usually a seat, like a hard seat.

59
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:12,680
But there are oftentimes special cars on a train where you can sleep for these overnight trips.

60
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:13,360
What are those called?

61
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:13,920
That's right.

62
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:18,080
And those are called sleeper cars or you choose a sleeper seat.

63
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:18,700
Right.

64
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:19,900
So that's pretty easy to remember.

65
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:22,680
So you choose a sleep sleeper.

66
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:26,120
The sleeper seats are the seats that fold back like you're lying down.

67
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:33,460
But of course most places like China and India and Vietnam for example have actual cars that have beds in them.

68
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:34,100
Right, right.

69
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:35,840
Yeah.

70
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,760
So you can, as the name says, you can actually sleep on this train.

71
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:45,320
Now if you're going to buy tickets, you're not going to find a sleeper train on an express train or a train that travels during the day.

72
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:47,000
These are only available at night usually.

73
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:47,620
That's right.

74
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:52,600
So a sleeper car is what you want to look for if you're traveling overnight.

75
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,200
And in those cases, you oftentimes get hungry.

76
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:56,200
You need to eat.

77
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:57,400
So where do you go to eat?

78
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,400
That's right.

79
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,480
We're going to look for the dining car on the train.

80
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:01,480
Okay.

81
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,440
So think about the word dining room.

82
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:05,930
Dining room, dining car, this means the place where you eat.

83
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:06,830
That's right.

84
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:14,410
So we're going to look for a dining car and it's usually in the front of the train I think and you can just go and get a quick meal over there.

85
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:16,260
That's right.

86
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:17,260
Dining car.

87
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:19,110
And finally, we have another very important word here.

88
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:21,490
It's all about getting on and off the train.

89
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:22,410
So where do you do that?

90
00:04:22,890 --> 00:04:27,890
Well, you go to the platform, the specific platform where your train is leaving.

91
00:04:28,190 --> 00:04:31,730
So in this case, this guy has to go to platform number nine.

92
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:32,640
Okay.

93
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:38,800
So usually that means that there is a cement or a stone block where you stand.

94
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:40,680
The train comes to the station.

95
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:42,360
People get off, you get on.

96
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:44,720
So the place where that all happens is the platform.

97
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:45,420
That's right.

98
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:51,560
And remember that in airplanes or in airports, usually you look for a terminal.

99
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:53,500
For train stations, you're looking for a platform.

100
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:54,340
All right.

101
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:55,780
And that number is going to be very important.

102
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,300
So look at your ticket and find out which platform your train will be arriving or departing from.

103
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:03,040
That's right.

104
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:04,040
Okay.

105
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:05,200
So now why don't we review these words.

106
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:09,720
Let's listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back in a bit.

107
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:15,320
Hi, I would like to purchase a one-way ticket to Brussels, please.

108
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:16,320
Certainly, sir.

109
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:17,280
This is our train schedule.

110
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,620
We have an express train departing every morning and an overnight train that departs at 9 p.m.

111
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:24,400
How long does it take to get there?

112
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:25,760
About 12 hours.

113
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:30,080
We currently have tickets available only for first class on the express train.

114
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:34,500
If you'd like, you can choose a sleeper on the overnight train, which is a bit less expensive.

115
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:36,860
Yeah, I think that's the best option.

116
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:38,420
Do you serve food on a train?

117
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:40,160
12 hours is such a long time.

118
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:41,200
Yes, of course.

119
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:44,920
There is a dining car towards the end of the train where they serve meals at all times.

120
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:48,400
We do provide complimentary water and coffee for all of our passengers.

121
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:49,220
Great.

122
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:50,000
I'll take it.

123
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:50,940
Here you are, sir.

124
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:54,360
Your train leaves from platform number 9 at 9 on the dot.

125
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:59,480
Remember to be here at least 30 minutes before your scheduled departure time or else you might miss your train.

126
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:00,220
I understand.

127
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:01,780
Thank you very much.

128
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:02,680
Have a great trip.

129
00:06:07,690 --> 00:06:08,150
Okay.

130
00:06:08,190 --> 00:06:13,130
So we've got some really great phrases today on Fluency Builder, not that many of them but they're very useful.

131
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:17,280
The first one is a kind of ticket you can buy.

132
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:18,280
That's right.

133
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:20,090
We're looking for a one-way ticket to Brussels.

134
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:21,220
Okay.

135
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:25,600
So a one-way ticket means you go to Brussels but you don't come home.

136
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,300
Maybe you have a flight later or you have a car waiting for you.

137
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:32,700
You don't need a return ticket on that train.

138
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:38,280
One-way ticket is the opposite of a round-trip ticket.

139
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:38,450
That's right.

140
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:48,400
So usually, for example, if you live in New York and you're going on vacation, you're going to buy a round-trip ticket because you need to go back to your city.

141
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:50,190
But in this case, we're getting a one-way ticket.

142
00:06:50,410 --> 00:06:52,830
So we're just buying a ticket that will only take us there.

143
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,090
It's not going to bring us back.

144
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:55,640
Okay.

145
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:56,960
One-way ticket.

146
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:59,190
And after that, we have a good question.

147
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:02,330
This is something we can ask in many, many different situations.

148
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:07,800
Here the person buying the ticket wants to know how long does it take to get there.

149
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:08,800
He's talking about Brussels.

150
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:09,920
That's right.

151
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:12,400
So let's take a look at this structure.

152
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:20,400
We can say how long does it take to and then we can add another verb there.

153
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:25,020
So for example, we want to know the amount of time it is going to require.

154
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:29,850
For example, here we are asking how long does it take to get there, in this case to Brussels.

155
00:07:30,620 --> 00:07:35,920
We're asking how much time is needed to go to Brussels, but what if we change the last part?

156
00:07:35,950 --> 00:07:36,610
Well, there's anything.

157
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:37,990
We could say anything here.

158
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:41,590
We could say how long does it take to bake a pizza?

159
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:50,880
Or for example, Marco, how long does it take for you to do your hair in the morning?

160
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:52,090
Like 20 seconds.

161
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:53,960
Not very long.

162
00:07:53,960 --> 00:07:54,960
Not very long.

163
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:56,150
You could even change the name of the place you're going to.

164
00:07:56,330 --> 00:07:58,710
So how long does it take to get to Beijing?

165
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:05,030
Exactly, so just remember this structure when you want to ask the amount of time needed.

166
00:08:05,330 --> 00:08:14,150
Of course, there are other ways and sometimes it's very common to make a little bit of mistakes saying how much time to Beijing or something like this.

167
00:08:14,270 --> 00:08:15,130
Or how many hours.

168
00:08:15,510 --> 00:08:16,630
We don't really say that in English.

169
00:08:16,710 --> 00:08:17,950
We say how much time.

170
00:08:18,110 --> 00:08:20,270
So how much time does it take?

171
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:21,630
All right.

172
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:31,970
And what about when the person that's selling the ticket said, okay, the train leaves from platform number nine at nine on the dot?

173
00:08:32,510 --> 00:08:37,710
Okay, so I'm not really sure where this idea of a dot comes from, but I think it has to do with clocks.

174
00:08:38,510 --> 00:08:47,670
So basically when you look at a clock and all of a sudden the hand comes to 12, so it's nine o'clock, the hand is on the 12, that is on the dot.

175
00:08:47,850 --> 00:08:48,850
That means exactly.

176
00:08:49,270 --> 00:08:55,370
Okay, so the train is leaving at nine zero zero, not nine 30, not nine 15.

177
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:58,080
It's leaving exactly at nine o'clock.

178
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:01,530
So we can also say this in normal speech when we're talking to our friends.

179
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:04,750
I could say, Marco, we're going to leave at six on the dot.

180
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:06,650
Any later and we'll miss the movie.

181
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:08,840
Exactly.

182
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:14,760
But when you say on the dot, you're usually emphasizing that it leaves promptly.

183
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:19,150
It's not going to be late and you're kind of emphasizing a person to be on time.

184
00:09:19,330 --> 00:09:26,050
Yes, please don't be late because, so nine oh one or in my case, six oh four, these are not okay because they're too late.

185
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:27,440
Exactly.

186
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:29,880
So I'm going to pick you up at four o'clock on the dot.

187
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:32,650
Usually means I don't really want to wait, so please be ready at four.

188
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:35,120
All right, so on the dot.

189
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:41,440
All right, so now let's listen to our dialogue for the last time and we'll be back to talk with you a little bit more.

190
00:09:47,570 --> 00:09:50,130
Hi, I would like to purchase a one-way ticket to Brussels, please.

191
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:51,270
Certainly, sir.

192
